Re-fetch live data and update today's daily note in place. Use this any time the note is stale — mid-morning after meetings, after lunch, before the commute, or when you said something to Claude that referenced data that turns out to be old.

Arguments: $ARGUMENTS

Argument forms:
- No args: rerun Track A in full (all rendered sections), Track C (the TriageList — inbox items + conversations, since your last marker), and Track C2 (both remaining commentary fragments — Morning read, Pulse asides). Track B (coffee/commute/catch-all) is **never** rerun by `/checkin` — see below.
- `email`, `whatsapp`, `conversations`, `calendar`, `transport`, `house`, `health`, `coffee`, `attachments`, `inbox`, `weather`, `listening`, `snags` — refresh only that section's `rendered` fragment. `email`/`whatsapp`/`conversations` all rerun the `## Conversations` section (Track C's List 2 — see below); `health`/`listening` additionally rerun their matching Track C2 fragment — see the table below and "Track C2 rerun" below.
- `morning` — rerun Track C2's `morning_read` fragment only.
- `intake`, `conversations` — rerun Track C (the TriageList) only.
- `--full`: rebuild every `rendered` section from scratch rather than trusting the hourly prewarm cache (`refresh: true` on the brief call). Use when the day's shape has fundamentally changed (a session got cancelled, a major new email landed).

The note is always your own — `vault/{{daily_notes_dir}}<today>.md`. There is no argument for refreshing another person's note; each user runs their own `/checkin`.

If today's note doesn't exist yet, this command bails out with a one-liner: *"No daily note for today. Run `/kickoff` first."* — that's deliberate. `/checkin` is an *update* command; creation goes through `/kickoff`.

## Claims carry their source

Same as `/kickoff`: everything here is pasted from `system_daily_brief(render=true)`'s `rendered` fragments, each already sourced at generation time — nothing to mark up further. **The note being refreshed is not a data source for anything new** — you may compare against what's already in a section to decide whether a rewrite is even needed (step 3), but never pull a figure from the note itself. If a re-fetched section now has no data for something the note currently shows, replace it with "not reported" rather than leaving the old content in place.

Track C2's two remaining fragments (Morning read, Pulse asides) are commentary, sourced the same restricted-payload way `/kickoff`'s Track C2 is (see step 4.5) — same rule: never restate a number a rendered fact fragment already carries, and never source anything from the note being refreshed. **Track C2's Email/WhatsApp digest fragments are retired (2026-09-10, lios#192/#193)** — `## Conversations` is now built from Track C's TriageList List 2 (`conversations_since`), same as `/kickoff`; see step 4 and "Track C rerun", below.

## Why Track B never reruns

Commute and the morning catch-all don't go stale within a day, and a second coffee is a "just tell me" rather than a standing prompt. If the user wants to log a second coffee, or add to the catch-all, take it conversationally and append it directly — don't re-ask the three questions.

## Steps

### 1. Locate the note

`vault/{{daily_notes_dir}}<today>.md`. If absent → bail with the message above.

### 2. Resolve which sections to refresh

**One call does almost all of it**, same shape as `/kickoff`'s Track A:

```
system_daily_brief {"render": true, "sections": [...], "since": "<previous note mtime ISO>", "refresh": true}
```

Pass `refresh: true` — a refresh is explicitly asking for current data, so the brief's hourly-prewarm cache should be bypassed for the requested sections (`--full` bypasses it for *every* section; a plain unfiltered refresh still benefits from the cache where an individual source hasn't changed — the server decides that, not this template).

Map the user's filters onto `rendered` keys:

| Filter | `sections` value | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `calendar` | `calendar` | |
| `weather` | *(none)* | folded into `pulse`/`consumables` context server-side, no separate fragment |
| `transport` | `transport` | only render it into the note if the note currently shows a Transport heading (i.e. this morning's commute answer was yes) — a refresh doesn't re-ask Track B |
| `email`, `whatsapp`, `conversations` | *(none — Track C only)* | all three rerun Track C's TriageList List 2 (`conversations_since`) and rebuild `## Conversations` — see step 4. `email`/`whatsapp` are kept as filter names for muscle memory; they no longer map to a separate rendered fragment or a separate Track C2 pass |
| `health`, `listening` | `pulse`, `listening` | `health` also reruns Track C2's `pulse_asides`; `listening` reruns just the `pulse_asides.listening` key |
| `morning` | *(none — Track C2 only)* | reruns `morning_read` only, no rendered-fragment refetch |
| `coffee` | `coffee` | |
| `house` | `consumables` | the only surviving piece of the old House section |
| `snags` | `snags` | also triggers the `snag_capture` write below |
| `inbox` | *(handled outside the brief, see 3.5)* | also rebuilds Track C's List 1 — see step 4 |
| `intake` | *(handled outside the brief, see 4)* | reruns Track C (both lists) |

No args → request every section the user's `daily_note.sections` preference includes, same as `/kickoff`.

The server accepts both this table's `rendered` names and the preference's own names (`today`, `house`) in `sections`. **Check `_meta.sections_unknown` is `[]` and `_meta.sections_enabled` is non-empty before pasting anything** — an empty `sections_enabled` means nothing was fetched and every fragment will read `_Not measured today._`; that's a bad request, so fix the names and call again.

System alerts (`rendered.alerts`, under its own `## System Alerts` heading) come back on **every** call regardless of filter — if something's degraded you need to know before trusting the rest of the refresh. It's never `""`; if the alerts source itself is unavailable it renders `_Not measured today._` rather than being omitted.

**A `rendered.<key>` value of `""` means the section's heading is dropped from the note on this refresh** — whether that's "not configured" or a genuine zero (no snags, no coffee, nothing nearly empty). `_Not measured today._` is a third state and keeps its heading — the source ran, it just has nothing for today. Never confuse the two, and never carry a heading forward from before this refresh just because the fresh fetch came back empty.

**If the note has a `## Data freshness` heading, rewrite it on every refresh** — whatever the filters, even when nothing else changed. A freshness table stamped 07:40 sitting under a section refreshed at 14:20 is worse than no table. If the note has no such heading, don't add one — the user has it switched off (`daily_note.show_freshness`).

**Also run `python3 .tools/vault_guard.py` on every refresh, regardless of filters** — cheap, local, instant. Exit 0 → nothing to do. Exit 1 → if the note has no `## Vault guard` heading, insert one (right after the top section) naming every finding verbatim; if it already has one, rewrite it with current findings; if a previously-reported finding is now gone, remove the heading entirely.

Three things sit outside the brief and are called alongside it, in the same parallel block, when relevant:

- `inbox_pending` — for the `inbox` filter (and unfiltered runs).
- `conversations_since(since=<the caller's own intake marker or the previous note mtime, ISO>)` — for `intake`/`email`/`whatsapp`/`conversations` (and unfiltered runs). Same deterministic call as `/kickoff`'s Track A — see step 4.
- `snag_capture` — for the `snags` filter (and unfiltered runs). It **writes** (idempotent against its own prior captures, default `since_days: 7`), which is why it isn't in the read-only brief. Rebuild `## Snags` from the result (omit the heading if `snags_created` is 0 this run — not cumulative for the day).

### 3. Edit the note in place

For each refreshed section, locate its heading (`## Today`, `## Pulse`, `## House` for `consumables`, `## Listening` — its own top-level heading, not nested inside Pulse — `## Conversations` for Track C's List 2, etc.; whatever heading the `rendered` fragment itself carries) and replace the whole fragment. Mark refreshed sections with a small `*(refreshed HH:MM)*` italic tag next to the heading — replace a previous tag rather than piling them up.

`## Snags`, `## Transport` and `## Vault guard` are conditional headings, present only when there's something to show — insert them fresh if the note predates them, remove them if this refresh finds nothing.

If a section's content hasn't materially changed (same fragment, byte for byte), still update the timestamp tag but don't otherwise touch it.

### 3.5. Inbox triage (conditional)

Only fires if `inbox_pending` returned `count > 0` and the user either passed no filters or passed `inbox` explicitly.

Identical shape to `/kickoff`'s inbox routing: numbered table (age/kind/source/original-filename/preview), one-line-per-item routing reply, then `inbox_to_corpus` / `inbox_to_vault` / `inbox_archive` / `inbox_dismiss` in parallel — including the `issue [bug|feature|other] [title…]` destination (`gh issue create -R cograda/lios`, then `inbox_archive`) for anything that's about lios itself rather than a household matter. `skip` leaves everything pending for next time.

### 4. Triage (Track C rerun)

Runs on a plain `/checkin` and on `/checkin intake`/`/checkin email`/`/checkin whatsapp`/`/checkin conversations`; the `inbox`-only filter reruns just List 1 (below); skipped entirely for any other single-section filter unless explicitly named.

Same TriageList shape as `/kickoff`'s Track C (see that command's file for the full instructions) — one subagent (Haiku), given `inbox_pending` and `conversations_since(since=<the caller's own intake marker>)` verbatim, which after `/kickoff` this morning means "since then," not "since yesterday." It builds **List 1** (inbox items, only if the `inbox` filter or an unfiltered run) and **List 2** (conversation groups labelled FYI/DECIDE, using `tasks_intake_candidates()` only to tag already-ledgered groups — never presented as its own list), continuous numbering across both, exactly as `/kickoff`'s Track C does. Present the same combined table and ask the same combined question (inbox verbs for List 1, `task`/`note`/`ignore`/`skip` for List 2's Decide rows).

For each confirmed task (from either list), check first whether it's clearly about lios itself (a bug or improvement in Loops, the daily note, a sync daemon, an MCP tool) — if so, file it as a GitHub issue (`gh issue create -R cograda/lios --title "<title>" --label <bug|feature|other> --body "<candidate text>, plus 'Captured via inbox intake <date>'"`) instead of `tasks_add`; the ledger is for household work only. Then `tasks_intake_mark(until=<the until Track C returned>)` regardless of how many were added.

Rebuild `## Conversations` from List 2's result exactly as `/kickoff` does (FYI rows collapsed to one line, Decide rows with their verdict) — mark the heading `*(refreshed HH:MM)*` per step 3's rule. Skipped-row persistence is the same deferred piece noted in `/kickoff` — not built yet.

If there's nothing new in either list, say so in one line and don't render a table.

### 4.5. Track C2 rerun (Morning read / Pulse asides)

Fires on a plain `/checkin`, `--full`, or any of `morning`, `pulse`, `health`, `listening`; skipped for every other single-section filter (`calendar`, `transport`, `coffee`, `house`, `snags`, `inbox`, `intake`, `email`, `whatsapp`, `conversations`) unless one of the trigger filters is also named. **`email`/`whatsapp`/`conversations` no longer trigger Track C2** — they trigger Track C (step 4) instead, since 2026-09-10.

Same subagent shape as `/kickoff`'s Track C2 (see that command's file for the full instructions) — a Sonnet subagent reading the same restricted ~75 KB slice of the just-refetched brief (`health_summary`, `health_sleep`, `health_trends`, `health_workouts`, `lastfm_recent`, `lastfm_stats`, `calendar`, `reminders`, `attachments`, `alerts.issues`), producing whichever of `morning_read` / `pulse_asides` this refresh actually needs (both on an unfiltered run or `--full`; otherwise only the fragment the trigger filter maps to per the table above). Track B's answers from this morning are still valid (they don't go stale within a day — see "Why Track B never reruns" above) — pass them along if this session still has them; if this is a fresh session with no memory of them, proceed without rather than re-asking, since `/checkin` never re-asks Track B's questions.

Replace the matching fragment(s) in the note in place, same rule as step 3: mark the touched heading(s) with `*(refreshed HH:MM)*`, and never touch a fragment this refresh didn't request. A `""` result omits the heading (or the aside line, for `pulse_asides`) exactly as it would on `/kickoff`; a one-line "not available" notice keeps it.

### 5. Update the Notes section delta

At the bottom of `## Notes`, append a one-paragraph delta of anything genuinely new since the last refresh — new intake items added, a snag captured, a section that changed materially. Skip this if nothing changed; don't pad it.

### 6. Confirm

One line: `"Refreshed: pulse, coffee, consumables, snags (1 captured), morning read, conversations. Triage: 2 added, 1 skipped."` — name only what actually changed.

## Never invokes `/tunetasks`

Confirmed in the plan of record: running the ledger audit on every intraday refresh is wasted churn. `/tunetasks` is a deliberate, once-a-day (or as-needed) act, not something a `/checkin` should trigger as a side effect.

## Why a separate command

`/kickoff` stays creation-shaped: fan out once, ask the human things, write the note from scratch. `/checkin` stays update-shaped: re-fetch, diff, edit sections in place. Filter args (`/checkin transport` before the commute) are fast and intent-clear.

*(This command was called `/refresh` until 2026-09-10, when it was renamed to `/checkin` — no behaviour change, lios#194.)*

## Safety rules

- Never overwrite handwritten user notes inside a section — append rather than replace if unsure.
- Never touch Focus — that's `/tunetasks`' job now, not a refresh.
- Never edit sections the user didn't ask for when filters are passed.
- If a filter is unrecognised, list valid filters and bail.
- Never source a number, count, or status claim from the note being refreshed — only from the fresh `rendered` fragment (or, for Morning read/Pulse asides, the fresh Track C2 subagent output; or, for Conversations, the fresh Track C TriageList).
